    {
      "to": "amara@finchdata.co",
      "subject": "Re: jac-cloud on Kubernetes?",
      "message": "Hi Amara,\n\nThanks for reaching out \u2014 happy to clarify both points.\n\n1. **Self-managed Kubernetes (EKS):** Yes, jac-cloud supports deployment on your own Kubernetes cluster, including EKS. jac-cloud ships as a containerized application, so you can run it on any standard Kubernetes distribution. A Helm chart is available to streamline deployment and configuration on your cluster. This should fit cleanly into your Q4 infrastructure plans.\n\n2. **Horizontal scaling of walkers:** jac-cloud does not auto-scale walkers out of the box \u2014 you configure scaling yourself using standard Kubernetes mechanisms, typically a HorizontalPodAutoscaler (HPA) on the walker-serving deployment. Because graph state is externalized in a shared database, walker replicas are stateless and scale independently with no coordination overhead between pods. You simply set your HPA thresholds (CPU, memory, or custom metrics) and Kubernetes handles the rest.\n\nLet me know if you'd like details on the Helm chart configuration or HPA setup specifics. Happy to help with your Q4 planning.\n\nBest,\nJaseci Team"
    },
